{
  "dom_id": "step-q20875",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{2}{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x=$",
  "worked_solution": "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int {e}^{px+q}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{p}{e}^{px+q}+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{2}{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{2}{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{2}]}_{a}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{e}^{2x}]}_{a}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot ({e}^{4}-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{2a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={e}^{2x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{e}^{2x}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{2x}=\\infty $\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/assets/b350aaef1440524297a84168de46be1171b7686d9937ef5bbfc5cd55835ea54f.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{2}{e}^{2x} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot ({e}^{4}-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{2a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot ({e}^{4}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{4}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
